Braintrust discovered the incident on 2026-05-04 after a report of suspicious behaviour ([SecurityWeek, 2026-05-08](https://www.securityweek.com/ai-firm-braintrust-prompts-api-key-rotation-after-data-breach/)) and disclosed it on its website on 2026-05-05 ([TechCrunch, 2026-05-06](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/06/ai-evaluation-startup-braintrust-confirms-breach-tells-every-customer-to-rotate-sensitive-keys/)); 2026-05-04 is not the date of the attackers' access, which no source gives, so there is no known access window to search audit logs against. The exposed secrets were the org-level API keys customers use to access AI models, with no AI provider named, and the email asked every customer to rotate them ([TechCrunch, 2026-05-06](https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/06/ai-evaluation-startup-braintrust-confirms-breach-tells-every-customer-to-rotate-sensitive-keys/)). Box, Cloudflare, Dropbox, Notion, Ramp and Stripe are companies whose AI provider keys were likely stored in Braintrust, in the estimate of Nudge Security's CTO, not SaaS services whose credentials were at risk ([SecurityWeek, 2026-05-08](https://www.securityweek.com/ai-firm-braintrust-prompts-api-key-rotation-after-data-breach/)). At least one customer was affected and three others reported suspicious spikes in AI provider usage ([SecurityWeek, 2026-05-08](https://www.securityweek.com/ai-firm-braintrust-prompts-api-key-rotation-after-data-breach/)), so the check that matters is the provider-side usage of the keys that were stored in Braintrust.
